    {
      "executionState" : "enabled",
      "id" : "note",
      "kind" : "edit.markdownNote",
      "parameters" : {
        "text" : "# Flurry · Five coupled smoke streams\nThe generic GPU Program holds 3,600 smoke particles and a half-float frame history. A slow moving star emits two particles into each of five streams per 60 Hz frame. The star and five sparks are invisible guides: every particle is attracted to all sparks, with stronger attraction to its assigned spark. Its color comes from that spark at birth. Only soft motion-blurred smoke quads are drawn, then their light is added to the faded previous frame. A final pass maps the accumulated light to sRGB for Image Output. Width and Height control both rendering and saved images; changing either restarts the simulation. FlurryProgram.metal sits beside this workflow through the Source File port. This is an independently authored reconstruction informed by Calum Robinson’s public Flurry source."
      },
      "resources" : {

      },
      "title" : "How the effect is built"
    },
